-- title:
--   Lemma 6.10 — power-series representable ε-close shrinking intervals in MDPs
-- statement:
--   For $p\in\{1,2\}$, let $P$ be a player-$p$ MDP with a reward function (values in $[0,1]$) and a succinct discount factor $\lambda=1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}}$, and let $B_P$ and $B_{\mathrm{sep}}$ be as in Lemma 6.9. Let $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}\in(0,1)$ and
--   $$\ell_{\mathrm{bnd}}=(n+1)(B_\varepsilon+\lceil\log(n+1)\rceil+8)+1,\qquad B_{\mathrm{bnd}}=B_{\mathrm{sep}}+B_\varepsilon+\lceil\log(n+1)\rceil+10.$$
--   Then there exists an $\varepsilon$-close shrinking interval bound $I=(L,U)$ of $P$ with $L(s),U(s)\in F(\ell_{\mathrm{bnd}},B_{\mathrm{bnd}})$ for all states $s$.
--
--   These are the polynomial-size interval certificates of the witness.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: a player-p MDP P = (S, A, δ) for p ∈ {1, 2} → a game with the other player's action set Fin 1 (CSG n m 1 for p = 1, CSG n 1 m for p = 2); the theorem is the conjunction of the two cases; reason: the source defines a player-p MDP as the special CSG where the other player has one action
--   - encoding: ⌈log x⌉ (base 2) → Nat.clog 2 x; reason: equal for every positive integer x; all logarithm arguments are positive because the state and action sets are nonempty
--   - encoding: B_P with a common denominator at most 2^{2^{B_P}}; ε = 2^{-Bε} ∈ (0, 1) → for every positive integer Q ≤ 2^{2^{B_P}} that is a common denominator; Bε ≥ 1; reason: equivalent hypotheses
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Lemma 6.10, printed p. 26 (PDF p. 28)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_PowerSeries
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Lemma 6.10 (Power-series representable interval bounds in MDPs).** For a player-`p` MDP `P`
(`p ∈ {1,2}`) with a reward function and succinct discount factor `λ = 1 - 2^{-2^{Bλ}}`, with
`B_P` and `B_sep` as in Lemma 6.9, `ε = 2^{-Bε} ∈ (0,1)`,
`ℓ_bnd = (n+1)(Bε + ⌈log(n+1)⌉ + 8) + 1` and `B_bnd = B_sep + Bε + ⌈log(n+1)⌉ + 10`, there is an
`ε`-close shrinking interval bound `(L, U)` with `L(s), U(s) ∈ F(ℓ_bnd, B_bnd)` for all states. -/
theorem lemma_6_10 :
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n m 1) (r : Reward n m 1), IsReward r → ∀ (Blam BP Q : ℕ), 0 < Q →
      Q ≤ 2 ^ (2 ^ BP) → HasCommonDenom P r Q → ∀ Bε : ℕ, 1 ≤ Bε →
        ∃ L U : Fin n → ℝ, IsShrinking₁ P r (succinct Blam) L U ∧
          IsClose ((1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ Bε) L U ∧
          ∀ s, L s ∈ F ((n + 1) * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 8) + 1)
                  ((Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1) + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 10) ∧
               U s ∈ F ((n + 1) * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 8) + 1)
                  ((Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1) + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 10)) ∧
    (∀ {n m : ℕ} (P : CSG n 1 m) (r : Reward n 1 m), IsReward r → ∀ (Blam BP Q : ℕ), 0 < Q →
      Q ≤ 2 ^ (2 ^ BP) → HasCommonDenom P r Q → ∀ Bε : ℕ, 1 ≤ Bε →
        ∃ L U : Fin n → ℝ, IsShrinking₂ P r (succinct Blam) L U ∧
          IsClose ((1 / 2 : ℝ) ^ Bε) L U ∧
          ∀ s, L s ∈ F ((n + 1) * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 8) + 1)
                  ((Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1) + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 10) ∧
               U s ∈ F ((n + 1) * (Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 8) + 1)
                  ((Blam + BP + 2 * Nat.clog 2 n + 1) + Bε + Nat.clog 2 (n + 1) + 10)) := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
